nt to relax the  rules in line with certain stipulations  like the recent vaccination requirement  it's been a very difficult couple of  years  but with the high vaccination right it's  i think it's motivated people to get  vaccinated because we want to get out of  it more  you know and  i think that'll stand us in good stead  for the future  the city of melbourne was growing  rapidly before the pandemic now many  people have moved away captain jimmy  gantidis has only worked a total of  three weeks in the past 20 months we're  his only guests today  australia enjoyed revenues of around 28  billion euros a year from oversea  tourists before the pandemic began  but up until now non-citizens were not  allowed to enter the country except  under special circumstances  by new year i hope international travel  comes back  tourists come down people having parties  again  that's what we need people to be happy  to be free  to live life don't be scared  that's what we want back to the way it  was  meanwhile theo roosus would like to  reopen his indoor area to guests but one  thing worries him from now on he has to  check if his customers are vaccinated if  he doesn't he could face a fine of up to  70 thousand euros  uh the culture down here has never been  that way it's always doing our doors are  open uh wide open for everybody to come  and again i if i had a choice  i would accept vaccinated and  unvaccinated people that would be my  choice  in melbourne they hope that this time  the lockdown is now finally over  at the beginning of 2022  australia is expected to open up again  to tourists from europe and when it does  melbourne will welcome them with open  arms  but will those arms have been vaccinated  let's talk about australia's policies  with catherine bennett who heads the  epidemiology department at deakin  university's faculty of health in  melbourne  historically a country of petty  criminals and as we heard they're an  open-air prison according to some i mean  what sort of good or harm has has it  done locking up all those aussies for so  long  oh it's had very different effects on  you depending which state you live in  victoria's really had the hardest run  with this and the longest time in  lockdown of course and the most severe  lockdowns with also the highest fines  and penalties associated with it and so  you find people that actually are now  still even though we're opening up  scared to leave home because they think  if it requires that level of restriction  that they should still be worried and  there are others who were over locked  down a long time ago and and we saw that  in protests and so try to manage  expectations keep people in the middle  where we're still doing things safely  but also allowing for the fact that  we've got people with very different  views and very different experiences in  the population catherine what's your  assessment did xero covert work  well it kept our infection rates even in  victoria the most  troubled state in terms of waves of  infection we're still looking at the  infection rate of something like 1.5  percent of the population very low and  of course we kept our death rate  incredibly low by world standards so  that did work whether we did it all  uh as perfectly as we could i think we  need to do a lot of evaluation work to  really understand where we could have  pulled back a bit and still had that  same outcome but um  what has happened in this year is we  started off very slow with our vaccine  rollout probably partly because we did  have this almost complacency with those  international borders closed but once  the virus the delta variant came  on  on site and we had to deal with it and  knew you couldn't close it down easily  as we had previous variants that then  was a game changer and that then pushed  our vaccine roll out to now you know  amongst some of the highest rates in the  world from something that was very slow  starting that's been a good outcome and  that small percentage of unvaccinated  that's right there's still um in some  states and territories you know we're  getting down to very low numbers some  states less than five percent of people  12 and over um  our two most populous states victoria  and new south wales are looking to be  well under 10 of people 12 and uh 12 and  over vaccinated other states are a bit  slower  we are seeing in victoria that  two-thirds of our current new cases  are people who are unvaccinated so  we're hoping those numbers will be small  enough  and of the people remaining unvaccinated  that there will  have been a number of people who have  had the virus by now and and actually be  you know have some natural immunity so  hopefully that that division based on  vaccination status will become less  relevant as the numbers are very small  of people who don't have any immunity  and does it help that summer's there now  i mean the fact that uv  light is a a lot more abundant down  under  yeah well that's right and i think you  know the timing has worked for us where  we've rolled out just coming out of our  uh spring and in fact it means that at  the time we started to open up in new  south wales and victoria 50 percent of  people were still within the first  month or two of their second dose so  we've got that booster time where you  get the best  outcomes from your vaccine where you've  got the best protection from onward  transmission not just serious illness  but any infection so we've got the  benefit of that as we wind into summer  and as you say you know there's a lot of  things about summer including a tendency  to mix and meet outdoors more often to  use outdoor areas in in hospitality and  so i think we will like the rest of the  world enjoy  a slightly less  pressurized period over the summer  anyway and then we'll be looking at our  booster program going in to early 2022  for the general population as well and  that should set us up i think well for  autumn  catherine one more thing what about  protecting businesses we heard in that  report about all of these small  businesses and bigger ones two folding  during the pandemic  there has been a lot of support for  businesses trying to keep them going you  know we know that the stress um the  financial stress for the workers as well  as the business operators and owners is  enormous and there's a big public health  problem in its own right so there has  been a lot of work to try and keep  businesses holding together job keeper  programs so that staff could be paid and  so  that's the reality that will hit now  though as people start to open up we do  actually need people to be getting out  and and participating in retail and in  those businesses to get them back on  their feet so i think it's one thing to  survive it's another thing to actually  have a profitable business and so i'm  hoping that as we do open up over summer  we do it safely and that helps these  businesses get back on their feet  because it's been a devastatingly long  series of lockdowns i don't know what  the count is i think it's 270 days in  victoria so an extraordinarily long time  to try and survive without you know with  disrupted business disrupted schools so  much going on that we now have a long  recovery from  catherine bennett deakin university  great to have you on the show  thank you  germany's vaccine advisory committee  known as stico is now recommending covet  19 vaccine boosters for all adults  this comes as the number of infections  in germany hits a new record the  committee says the top-up shot should be  administered with either the modino or  fisa biontech vaccine it said a booster  should be given five to six months after  the last dose  time for our science correspondent derek  williams and a viewer question about  comparing vaccines  i am vaccinated with johnson johnson am  i less protected than if i had been  vaccinated with another vaccine  well that depends on which vaccine you  want to compare it to and straight up  comparisons are tricky territory anyway  so  in addressing this question i'm only  going to focus on what the centers for  disease control said about the topic in  a publication from september now in this  big study  the researchers didn't look at how  effective the three different vaccines  approved in the u.s were  at preventing breakthrough infections  overall and those who were vaccinated  instead  it took a stab at estimating risk of  hospitalization  when an otherwise  generally healthy vaccinated adult  somehow got infected with covet 19  anyway  and since risk of hospitalization has  become a really key metric  as our stretched health care systems  head into another pandemic winter  that makes sense to me i think that the  most important takeaway was that  although the vaccines approved in the us  provided varying levels of protection  against hospitalization  for all three  that protection was still considered  substantial  in percentage terms however vaccine  effectiveness was estimated at 71  percent for johnson and johnson and  around  90 percent for the two approved  double-dose messenger rna vaccines the  authors therefore said that the data  indicated  they provided more protection than the  single shot johnson and johnson vaccine  but they also hasten to add that there  are several limitations to the report  not least that as i said  it looked only at hospitalizations and  not overall vaccine effectiveness  another important thing to remember is  that in many countries  people who got the johnson and johnson  vaccine  are now being urged to get a booster  within a couple of months and and that  will almost certainly increase  protection and recipients  even more substantially at least  in the medium term  so nice to have you along stay safe and  see you again soon  [Music]  you

